WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.290
From a vague request to a paid, trusted analysis.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:10.900
This is the video for module 3 of SQL Fundamentals: Analysis Projects, Clients and Your Portfolio.

3
00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:20.640
A manager says, send me our best customers. If you start typing straight away, you answer your own guess, not her question. So ask first.

4
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.710
What decision will this support? For Kemi, choosing who gets a loyalty discount.

5
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.100
How do you define best? Highest delivered spend, not number of orders.

6
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.510
Which period? 1 May to 31 August 2026.

7
00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.790
What should be left out? Cancelled and pending orders.

8
00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:50.290
What format, by when, and is it one-off or monthly? Then write the definition back in one sentence and get a yes.

9
00:00:51.540 --> 00:01:02.720
A CTE, a common table expression, lets you name one step with WITH, then use it like a table. Kemi's first step, called spend, totals delivered orders per customer.

10
00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:10.310
Run that step alone. 5 customers, adding to 211,500. Good.

11
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:20.260
The average per buying customer is 211,500 divided by 5, which is 42,300.

12
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:30.770
The final query keeps customers above that: Dupe 61,000, Amaka 55,000, Bisi 54,500.

13
00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:40.190
Then say what the number hides. Dupe made one large purchase, while Amaka bought three times. That may change who gets which offer.

14
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:52.870
Window functions calculate across rows while keeping every row. SUM OVER gives a running total. LAG brings the value from the previous row, so you can see the change.

15
00:01:54.120 --> 00:02:05.170
May brought 69,500 naira. June brought 73,500, up 4,000, so the running total is 143,000.

16
00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:11.500
July fell to 60,500, and August shows only 8,000.

17
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:25.210
The final running total, 211,500, equals the delivered total, so it checks out. But August has only one order, so the honest note is that August may be incomplete.

18
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:43.120
Beginners price only the time to type the final query. Real work takes longer. Halima estimates every stage: discovery and scope, 2 hours. Access and loading, 3. Writing and testing queries, 5. Checks, 2. The report, 3.

19
00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:49.060
That is 15 hours. She adds a 20 percent buffer, 3 hours, for 18 in total.

20
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:56.500
At an illustrative 5,000 naira an hour, that is 90,000 naira. Research your own rate.

21
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:06.300
She asks for 50 percent, 45,000, as a deposit before access. And extra questions get a friendly add-on quote.

22
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:23.210
Sales data holds personal data, and the Nigeria Data Protection Act 2023 sets rules for it. So ask for read-only access. Before any command that changes data, first run the same WHERE condition in a SELECT.

23
00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:34.100
Ask only for the columns you need. You rarely need phone numbers to count sales. Never send exports through personal WhatsApp chats or public tools.

24
00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:41.110
Store files safely, delete them at the end of the job, and confirm the deletion in writing.

25
00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:54.420
Certificates say you studied. A portfolio shows you can do the work. Take one public dataset, from Kaggle or World Bank Open Data, and write 3 to 5 questions a manager would ask.

26
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.390
Write one query per question.

27
00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.160
Record the checks you ran, and what they caught.

28
00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.650
Write findings a non-technical reader could act on.

29
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:15.120
Then publish it with a README and pin it to LinkedIn. Over 90 days: practise daily, build the project, publish, then apply.

30
00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:29.510
To remember. Agree the definition before you query. Build in named, checked steps. Price every stage of the work, not just the final query. And treat client data with care from access to deletion.

31
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:38.160
Now run a discovery conversation, write a one-page scope and quote, and start your portfolio with a 90-day plan.

32
00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.450
Continue with the lessons below.
